#Exchange Permission Issue Fix for Distribution Groups (samtech.com)
#Environment:

- Active Directory domain: samtech.com
- Organizational Unit (OU): OU=Employees,DC=samtech,DC=com
- Exchange security principal: Exchange Trusted Subsystem

#Issue Description: Administrators are unable to create, modify, or manage distribution groups within the Employees OU in Active Directory. When attempting to 
perform operations such as creating a new distribution group, adding or removing members, or updating group properties throughExchange, the operation fails.

#Common error messages include:
- Access is denied
- Insufficient permissions to perform the operation
- Failed to update Active Directory object
- Unable to modify distribution group
- These errors occur even when the administrator account has Exchange admin privileges.

#Cause: This issue occurs because the Exchange Trusted Subsystem security group does not have the required permissions on the Employees OU in Active Directory.
#Exchange uses the Exchange Trusted Subsystem to perform background operations in Active Directory, such as:

- Creating distribution groups
- Modifying group membership
- Updating mail-enabled group attributes
If this group does not have sufficient rights on the OU where the distribution groups are stored, Exchange cannot perform these actions, resulting in permission errors.

#Resolution: To resolve this issue, the required permissions must be assigned to the Exchange Trusted Subsystem on the affected OU.

#Steps to Fix:
- Open Active Directory Users and Computers
- From the top menu, select View > Advanced Features to enable advanced security settings
- Navigate to the following OU:
- OU=Employees,DC=samtech,DC=com
- Right-click the Employees OU and select Properties
- Go to the Security tab and click Advanced
- Click Add to add a new permission entry
- Click Select a principal and enter:
- Exchange Trusted Subsystem
- Then click OK

#Set the permission scope:
- Applies to: Descendant Group objects
- This ensures the permission applies only to distribution groups inside the OU
- Scroll down and click Clear all to remove default permissions

#Under permissions, select:
- Modify permissions
- Click OK, then apply and close all open windows

# Result After Fix
Once the permissions are applied correctly:

- Exchange can create new distribution groups in the Employees OU
- Existing distribution groups can be modified without errors
- Group membership can be updated successfully
- Mail-enabled group attributes can be managed without access issues
- Permission-related errors in Exchange are resolved

# Conclusion: The issue is caused by missing Active Directory permissions for the Exchange Trusted Subsystem on the target OU. Granting Modify permissions on 
the Employees OU for this security group allows Exchange to fully manage distribution groups without restrictions.
